A monitoring script (cron_monitor.sh) that independently confirms the cron service is healthy, the expected jobs are scheduled, and the resulting log files are being populated as expected.
Conclusion
In this lab, students built a complete automated security workflow for a Linux system using bash: a port and service hardening script, a patch management script with backup and reboot handling, and a comprehensive security audit script that produces a structured report covering permissions, SUID/SGID files, user accounts, network exposure, and log analysis. Students then tied these scripts together with a master orchestration script and scheduled it using cron, and finally built a dedicated monitoring script to independently verify that the cron service, the scheduled jobs, and the resulting log files were all functioning correctly. Through this process, students practiced the core discipline of security automation: writing idempotent, logging-aware scripts, scheduling them reliably, and building verification tooling so that automated controls can be trusted rather than assumed to be working.
